DOC -- documentation for the freeware commands==============================================One subdirectory per package, holding whatever documentation that packageoriginally shipped with: manual pages, .doc files, release notes, and theupstream README.  Text is stored with OS-9 (CR) line endings, so it can beread in place with `cat` (or `less` to page), e.g.    cat  /dd/DOC/sh/sh.doc    less /dd/DOC/elvis/elvis.doc(Note `list` is a Microware command and is NOT on this disk -- cat and lessare the freeware readers here.)Where a package is under the GPL, its COPYING file is kept here too -- thatis a licence obligation for the binaries in CMDS, not a convenience.Two exceptions worth knowing: gcc139 carries its licence insidegcc.texinfo rather than a separate file, and sbprolog is under SUNY StonyBrook's own licence (DOC/sbprolog/COPYING), not the GPL.The index files, which are not package directories:  CATEGORIES   every program grouped by what it is FOR -- start here if               you do not yet know the name you want  INDEX        what every program IS, alphabetically, with its caveats  ORIGINS      which archive each program came out of  README-KSH   why ksh could not be typed at, and what fixes it  USAGE        what every program says when asked for its help: the               command that asked, and the whole answer.  Captured               from the programs, not composed  DEPENDS      what each program needs besides its own binary  MANPAGES     the manual pages on this disk, and where each one is --               `man' reads it  README-CIO   the starred programs, and how to supply cio  README-RUNNING   three ways to arrange /dd and /h0  README-ERRMSG    the error message file, and BASIC09's numbersThe package directories:  bash/     Bourne-Again Shell -- readme, OSK port notes  ed/       GNU ed 0.2 -- README, OSK port notes, ed.1, COPYING (GPL)  elvis/    Elvis 1.7 (vi clone) -- elvis/ctags/fmt/ref man pages + .doc            The program is CMDS/elvis, its source SRC/elvis; view and            vi.elvis are elvis under other names (DOC/README-VI).  find/     find 1.1.5 -- find.doc, find.man, release notes  gcc/      GCC 2.5.6 OS-9 port notes  grep/     GNU grep 2.0 -- README, OSK port notes, grep.man, COPYING (GPL)  ncurses/  ncurses -- installation, terminal and compile notes  sc/       sc spreadsheet -- manual and tutorial  sh/       Bourne shell v7.5 -- sh.doc, changelog, SCRIPTS/ examplesProvenance and per-command test status for everything in CMDS is in../SOURCES.txt.Docs extracted from the original .lzh archives kept in CMDS/archives/ --those archives also contain full C source for ed, elvis and grep, which isnot unpacked here.Files in this directory that are not per-package documentation:  INDEX                what is on this disk, section by section  ORIGINS              which archive each added program came from  STATUS               per-command test status  USAGE                captured usage text for the undocumented  EFFO-INFO            the EFFO forum disks' own info files, which carry                       each author's licence conditions -- read before                       redistributing anything from those disks  README-RUNNING       how to start things, and what to set first  README-DOCS          where each package's own documentation lives  README-CIO           the starred programs need Microware's cio --                       three ways to set that up  DEPENDS              per program, every absolute path its binary carries.                       Read this before copying single commands onto a disk                       of your own -- 110 need more than the binary.  README-FLOATINGPOINT math.l and math881.l give the same answers; link                       math881.l for a machine with a 68881  README-SRC-LS        SRC/ls DOES rebuild the ls on this disk, since                       2026-08-24, and what the four fixes were  README-GCC           the gcc toolchains need setup and your own SDK;                       read it before trying to compile anything  README-VI            two vi editors -- which to take, and what each                       needs besides itself  README-KERMIT        five kermits -- which one to take, and the two flags                       that decide whether a transfer works  README-GREP          six ways to search a file -- which one to take, and                       the two that are not drop-in replacements  README-ARCHIVERS     twenty archivers in seven formats -- which to pack                       with, which to unpack with, and the one that cannot  README-EDITORS       the eight editors that are NOT vi: SIX Emacsen and                       which to take, a line editor and a binary editor  README-SHELLS        the five shells, and why SYS/login names ksh  README-NEWS          three news systems -- UUCPbb's, C News's tools and                       MNews with nn -- which to take, and the one                       `rnews' they cannot share  README-NETPBM        the 169 image converters, and how to see them work  README-KEEP          taking part of this collection onto your own media  README-NAMES         twenty programs here carry the name of a utility                       you already have -- which, and what decides which                       one runs  README-MODULES       not everything in CMDS is a command -- drivers,                       descriptors and trap libraries live there too  README-BUSERR        programs that fault, and the trap of testing them                       bare  README-FORTRAN       the RTF/68K Fortran-77 system, and the one thing it                       needs  README-METAFONT      the eight bitmap-font tools, the base that now                       ships, and why no .pk file does  README-DVI           eleven DVI drivers, one per printer -- which to                       take, what each writes, and the font warning  README-NOT-SHIPPED   programs found in the archives that stayed out,                       why, and where each one is  NEEDCIO-INDEX        the older per-program notes on the starred programs